WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:13.240
Taiwan has become something like the Asian
version of Asterix and Obelix’s irreducible

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:14.240
village.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:18.310
A small enclave surrounded by a great empire
that wants to engulf it...

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:22.990
And as with the small Gallic village, the
citizens of Taiwan also have their own magic

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:26.110
potion to defend themselves against their
enemies....

00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:29.930
Of course, in this case we aren’t talking
about potions made by a Druid... we’re talking

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:33.000
about the power of the United States.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.040
Well... the United States... and also, recently,
something else.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:43.680
Folks, Taiwan has a new ally, a new secret
advantage that is already fueling Beijing’s

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:45.280
wrath.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:48.490
Listen up.

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:51.690
(BEIJING’S WRATH)

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:57.910
Taiwan, is facing enormous pressure from mainland
China, which, as you know, claims the island

00:00:57.910 --> 00:00:58.910
as part of its territory.

00:00:58.910 --> 00:01:03.450
And, we’re not talking about the usual international
politics tug of war...

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:11.200
You give it back, you take it, I get angry,
I threaten you, then we let it be.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:13.360
Not at all...

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:17.710
Beijing has even set a deadline:

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:22.470
See, the Chinese government has made it clear,
time and time again, that it’s ready to

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:27.780
do what it takes, and that before the year
2049 – the centennial of the People's Republic

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:34.159
of China – Taiwan will have to rejoin mainland
China.

00:01:34.159 --> 00:01:35.159
Of course...

00:01:35.159 --> 00:01:36.909
this leads to a problem...

00:01:36.909 --> 00:01:41.479
and not exactly a small one: the Taiwanese
aren’t really in favor of it.

00:01:41.479 --> 00:01:45.130
They want nothing to do with it.

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:49.640
In fact, as we mentioned here on VISUALPOLITIK,
in 2016 the Democratic Progressive Party of

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:54.390
Tsai Ing-Wen – who by the way, was one of
our 2017 award-winning leaders of the year

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:59.280
– and who is rather an independentist, gained
the power in Taiwan, all the power... both

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.190
of Congress and the presidency.

00:02:01.190 --> 00:02:05.799
And since then... let's say that Beijing hasn’t
taken this well.

00:02:05.799 --> 00:02:10.840
What the hell... they took it horribly.

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:12.730
Since Tsa Ing-Wen became President...

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:16.500
practically from the very first minute, Beijing's
pressures against the island multiplied...

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:20.870
and in recent months it’s already reached
politically obsessive levels.

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:25.550
They’ve cut diplomatic ties, tried to punish
Taiwan economically and have implemented huge

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:28.340
military exercises around the island.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:31.040
But that’s not all.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:35.730
Xi Jinping’s government is also using all
its means to erase Taiwan’s name and identity

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:36.730
from the map.

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:39.530
For example, when we published our first video
about Taiwan...

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:44.310
20 countries formally recognized the island’s
government in Taipei as China’s legitimate

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:45.310
government.

00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:48.590
Yes, as we’ve mentioned before, Taiwan has
never declared its independence...

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:51.950
but for years it’s been claimed under China’s
legitimate government.

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:59.570
And of course, diplomatically, a country can
only have one recognized government.

00:02:59.570 --> 00:03:06.280
Well, Beijing has multiplied its diplomatic
agenda to end this situation.

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:11.590
Today, just a year later, only 17 countries
consider it so, and almost all of them are

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:12.590
tiny.

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:13.590
But that’s not all.

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:16.640
In fact, given the circumstances, Beijing’s
move would be understandable.

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:19.290
Something like: “I’m the legitimate government
of China”.

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:24.480
But this is no longer a question of who’s
the legitimate government... but simply that

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:29.430
Beijing has said both actively and passively...
that it will never allow Taiwan to become

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:30.430
independent.

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:35.489
And one way to prevent that... is, precisely,
by trying to erase all traces of the island’s

00:03:35.489 --> 00:03:36.769
national identity.

00:03:36.769 --> 00:03:37.769
Taiwan?

00:03:37.769 --> 00:03:38.769
What’s Taiwan?

00:03:38.769 --> 00:03:41.420
A car brand?

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:47.159
See, in order to pull this off, Beijing is
blocking the presence of Taiwanese representatives

00:03:47.159 --> 00:03:51.240
– and even its journalists – from all
of the international bodies it can… such

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:53.300
as the World Health Organization.

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:57.569
It’s fining companies that sell products
in China with the “Made in Taiwan” label.

00:03:57.569 --> 00:04:00.340
Which for example happened to the Japanese
chain Muji.

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:04.209
Another case we know about is that of the
GAP clothing chain, which had to apologize

00:04:04.209 --> 00:04:08.680
for selling T-shirts in North America that
didn’t include Taiwan as part of China.

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:12.650
Even Taiwanese music groups like the indie
punk band Fire EX, who don’t support the

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:16.489
reunification, are disappearing from Chinese
streaming music services.

00:04:16.489 --> 00:04:17.489
And...

00:04:17.489 --> 00:04:18.849
Want one last example?

00:04:18.849 --> 00:04:19.849
Well...

00:04:19.849 --> 00:04:23.780
Due to Beijing’s threat, airlines from all
over the world are no longer using the island’s

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:24.780
name.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:25.800
Yes, you heard that right.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:28.820
Even American companies aren’t using Taiwan’s
name anymore.

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:34.020
I mean, yes, you can book a ticket from New
York to Taipei... but don’t ask which country

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:35.910
you’re going to.

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:40.500
Although yes, unlike other international companies
such as British Airways or Lufthansa, who

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:43.590
are already selling tickets to Taiwan as part
of China...

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:47.440
American companies have simply resorted to
the ghost country trick:

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:49.680
I sell tickets to…

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:50.680
Taipei.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:52.310
Where is that?

00:04:52.310 --> 00:04:53.620
Who cares?

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:59.580
And we can’t blame them… the Chinese aviation
market is too big... and many companies have

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:02.690
direct interests in the country.

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:05.500
( “China’s goal regarding Taiwan has never
changed.

00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:10.630
That goal is to swallow up Taiwan’s sovereignty,
exterminate Taiwanese democracy and achieve

00:05:10.630 --> 00:05:15.490
ultimate unification.” Lee Teng-hui, Taiwan’s
first democratically elected president to

00:05:15.490 --> 00:05:16.880
New York Times.)

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:18.270
Without going any further...

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:21.740
American Airlines is a shareholder of China
Southern Airlines and Delta, on the other

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:23.870
hand, of China Eastern Airlines.

00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:24.870
However...

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:28.220
just a moment... because it’s not all bad
news for Taiwan.

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:31.669
Along with the US’s support, the island
has gained another powerful ally…

00:05:31.669 --> 00:05:33.100
Or… you could say…

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:36.560
Taiwan is developing its own magic potion...

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:38.229
And no, I'm not talking about weapons.

00:05:38.229 --> 00:05:43.550
Listen up.

00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:51.060
(THE MECA OF FREEDOM)

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:57.370
Folks, Taiwan has been chosen as the first
Asian host of the Oslo Freedom Forum, something

00:05:57.370 --> 00:06:00.700
like a Congress, an international meeting
on human rights.

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:01.700
But...

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:02.700
some of you may be wondering...

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:03.700
A Congress?

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:05.300
Is that really so important?

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:07.360
Well, my friends, in fact it is.

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:11.720
NOT so much for the Congress, but ultimately
for what it means.

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:17.860
(“Even though global democracy has experienced
a general trend of backsliding in the past

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:22.490
few years, Taiwan’s democracy has persisted
and continues to thrive.

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:27.490
Taiwan can continue to serve as inspiration
to democrats in Asia, including those in China.”

00:06:27.490 --> 00:06:31.110
Ketty Chen, vice president of the Taiwan Foundation
for Democracy)

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:36.650
See, for decades, Hong Kong was the great
bastion of freedom of expression in this area

00:06:36.650 --> 00:06:38.389
of the world.

00:06:38.389 --> 00:06:43.259
Both international media and human rights
organizations established their headquarters

00:06:43.259 --> 00:06:44.259
there.

00:06:44.259 --> 00:06:47.170
And not only that, Hong Kong also became a
refuge for many political exiles... like,

00:06:47.170 --> 00:06:50.740
for example, the student leaders who led the
Tiananmen protests.

00:06:50.740 --> 00:06:55.570
However, in recent times and as we’ve seen
here on VISUALPOLITIK...

00:06:55.570 --> 00:06:56.600
things have changed…

00:06:56.600 --> 00:07:06.030
And Beijing has tightened its grip on Hong
Kong politics.

00:07:06.030 --> 00:07:15.410
(“Hong Kong was originally the first choice
for the Asia bureau.

00:07:15.410 --> 00:07:19.919
But today China doesn’t just suppress its
own people, it is now increasingly exporting

00:07:19.919 --> 00:07:21.680
that suppression to Hong Kong.”

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:28.050
Wu’er Kaixi, an emeritus member of Reporters
Without Borders, and a student leader of the

00:07:28.050 --> 00:07:29.990
1989 Tiananmen protests.)

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:32.310
But... as this change was taking place...

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:37.639
Taiwan consolidated itself as a vibrant democracy
and one of the freest places in the region.

00:07:37.639 --> 00:07:42.190
That’s precisely why it’s replacing Hong
Kong as the Mecca for freedom activists and

00:07:42.190 --> 00:07:44.530
human rights in Asia.

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:56.660
Even though until 1987, Taiwan was under the
control of an ironclad dictatorship.

00:07:56.660 --> 00:08:03.330
Now, for example, one of the human rights
film festivals that used to be held in Hong

00:08:03.330 --> 00:08:05.039
Kong moved to Taiwan.

00:08:05.039 --> 00:08:09.580
Lam Wing-Kee, one of the Hong Kong book publishers
who was kidnapped by Chinese government agents

00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:13.389
2 years ago, has announced that he will reopen
his bookstore in Taiwan.

00:08:13.389 --> 00:08:18.349
And Reporters Without Borders announced in
2017 that its first Asian office will be in

00:08:18.349 --> 00:08:19.349
Taipei.

00:08:19.349 --> 00:08:22.450
(“Freedom of expression is a big part of
Taiwanese culture.”

00:08:22.450 --> 00:08:27.190
Cédric Alviani, the Taipei bureau director
of Reporters Without Borders.)

00:08:27.190 --> 00:08:31.310
These are just some examples of what is already
becoming a generalized trend.

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:35.770
Democracy, political plurality, freedom of
expression, human rights, same-sex marriage...

00:08:35.770 --> 00:08:37.729
Taiwan is becoming a benchmark.

00:08:37.729 --> 00:08:41.789
And obviously this poses a huge problem for
China’s interests.

00:08:41.789 --> 00:08:44.529
For one, the Taiwanese love freedom...

00:08:44.529 --> 00:08:47.389
so, of course, they don’t want anything
to do with being ruled by Beijing's orders

00:08:47.389 --> 00:08:48.389
again.

00:08:48.389 --> 00:08:53.040
And for another, while the Chinese government
attempts to erase, to annihilate the Taiwanese

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:54.439
identity throughout the world...

00:08:54.439 --> 00:08:57.420
the country has suddenly emerged as a center
of freedom...

00:08:57.420 --> 00:09:00.220
with all the influence that entails.

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:05.949
Also, it’s much harder to consider attacking
a country that is famous for its commitment

00:09:05.949 --> 00:09:06.949
to human rights.

00:09:06.949 --> 00:09:09.509
The International Community wouldn’t accept
it.

00:09:09.509 --> 00:09:14.749
And the truth is that this may be Taiwan’s
best move.

00:09:14.749 --> 00:09:18.459
Listen up.

00:09:18.459 --> 00:09:24.190
(THE NEW MOVE)

00:09:24.190 --> 00:09:29.639
In mid-July 2018, the second Apache helicopter
squadron, made in Taiwan, went into service.

00:09:29.639 --> 00:09:33.529
It’s part of what is strengthening the partnership
between Washington and Taipei

00:09:33.529 --> 00:09:38.300
- Of which, incidentally, we’ll speak in
a future video -, which among other things

00:09:38.300 --> 00:09:42.420
includes a significant increase in the sale
of equipment and military technology to the

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:44.310
island.

00:09:44.310 --> 00:09:49.119
Taiwan is buying helicopters, tanks, missiles,
anti-aircraft systems and now even wants to

00:09:49.119 --> 00:09:50.850
develop its own submarines.

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:51.850
However...

00:09:51.850 --> 00:09:55.860
despite all this, it seems increasingly clear
that militarily, it’ll be difficult for

00:09:55.860 --> 00:09:57.389
Taiwan to stop China...

00:09:57.389 --> 00:10:00.870
the budget difference is overwhelming...

00:10:00.870 --> 00:10:06.290
China's military budget is between 15 and
20 times that of Taiwan... and it shows.

00:10:06.290 --> 00:10:12.689
Yes, it's true, nowadays there’s the United
States umbrella... but even that isn’t enough.

00:10:12.689 --> 00:10:16.700
To have the guarantee that the international
community would act in their favor, Taiwan

00:10:16.700 --> 00:10:22.389
needs to reinforce its influence in the world...
especially if it wants to become an independent

00:10:22.389 --> 00:10:23.389
country.

00:10:23.389 --> 00:10:29.070
Now, the question we need to ask ourselves
is how on earth can they achieve this?

00:10:29.070 --> 00:10:36.600
Well, folks, though it may surprise you, the
military strategy – while essential – may

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:39.550
not be the most important.

00:10:39.550 --> 00:10:43.600
Taiwan has to gain influence and prominence
in the world, while overcoming the economic

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:46.040
problems that we talked about in a previous
video.

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:51.160
To do so, it can, for example, seek to attract
students from all over Asia and around the

00:10:51.160 --> 00:10:55.320
world... by giving them facilities, promoting
programs in English and attracting first-rate

00:10:55.320 --> 00:10:56.320
teachers.

00:10:56.320 --> 00:11:00.480
That would mean gaining a lot of influence
and, of course, human capital.

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:04.850
Second, Taiwan can set in motion an aggressive
policy to attract multinationals and get them

00:11:04.850 --> 00:11:10.209
to establish not only factories here but,
above all, research centers and administrative

00:11:10.209 --> 00:11:11.209
headquarters.

00:11:11.209 --> 00:11:15.579
They can do so by devoting special attention
to attracting content creation companies:

00:11:15.579 --> 00:11:23.199
media, film studios, youtubers, etc., etcetera.

00:11:23.199 --> 00:11:29.089
Third... the government could seek to become
a logistic enclave... for example by developing

00:11:29.089 --> 00:11:32.700
its airport capabilities and thus becoming
a large communications hub.

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:35.779
Fourth, it can attract more professionals
from the rest of Asia...

00:11:35.779 --> 00:11:40.480
Which would allow it both to face the island’s
aging population, and, also, to strengthen

00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:43.240
its ties with the workers’ places of origin.

00:11:43.240 --> 00:11:47.889
And fifth, it must continue to promote its
role as a center for human rights and freedom

00:11:47.889 --> 00:11:48.889
of expression.

00:11:48.889 --> 00:11:54.449
Okay, it's true, maybe a plan like this isn’t
as exciting as huge weapons plans... but it

00:11:54.449 --> 00:11:58.910
would be much cheaper – as trying to compete
with China only via the military route seems

00:11:58.910 --> 00:12:03.149
complicated – and it would boost the island’s
economy as well as give it a lot of influence

00:12:03.149 --> 00:12:04.259
around the world.

00:12:04.259 --> 00:12:09.040
Given the circumstances, a university, a film
studio, a hotel or the headquarters of a human

00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:13.839
rights or multinational organization can be
much more important for the island’s survival

00:12:13.839 --> 00:12:18.100
than, for example, a battleship.

00:12:18.100 --> 00:12:22.440
And no, it's not about giving an ultra-pacifist
discourse or anything like that...

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:23.880
it's about pure pragmatism...

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:29.199
if we're talking about guaranteeing Taiwan's
independence and freedom.

00:12:29.199 --> 00:12:31.199
But now it's your turn.

00:12:31.199 --> 00:12:35.049
Do you think the International Community should
guarantee Taiwan’s security?

00:12:35.049 --> 00:12:37.499
Leave your answer in the comments as well
as in the survey.

00:12:37.499 --> 00:12:41.649
So I really hope you enjoyed this video, please
hit like if you did, and don’t forget to

00:12:41.649 --> 00:12:44.079
subscribe for brand new videos.

